---
import Layout from "@/components/Layout.astro";
import { getTimeline, propagation, meta } from "@/lib/load";
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/format";
import { statementPath, withBase } from "@/lib/paths";
import { getStatements } from "@/lib/statements";
import { FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL, REQUIREMENTS } from "@/lib/profile-labels";
import type { FieldSource } from "@/lib/profile-labels";
import ChangeFeedItem from "@/components/ChangeFeedItem.astro";

const sources: FieldSource[] = ["standard", "policy", "ai-plan", "tracker"];

const corpusStart = meta.corpusStart ? formatDate(meta.corpusStart) : "November 2025";

const tierCounts: Record<"added" | "present-at-start" | "tied", number> = {
  added: 0,
  "present-at-start": 0,
  tied: 0,
};
for (const c of propagation.clusters) {
  const t = c.firstObserved?.tier;
  if (t) tierCounts[t] += 1;
}

// The worked example: one small change that exercises both readings. The
// captions below describe this particular change, so fail the build rather
// than show them against a different one if a history rewrite loses it.
const EXAMPLE_ID = "FCA:0704351742";
const example = (await getTimeline()).find((e) => e.id === EXAMPLE_ID);
const exampleRevs = example ? (await getStatements())[example.statementId]?.timeline : undefined;
const exampleIdx = exampleRevs?.findIndex((r) => r.sha === example?.sha) ?? -1;
if (!example || !exampleRevs || exampleIdx < 1) {
  throw new Error(`reading: worked example ${EXAMPLE_ID} is missing from the export`);
}
const exampleBefore = exampleRevs[exampleIdx - 1];
const exampleAfter = exampleRevs[exampleIdx];
---

<Layout
  title="Reading the data"
  description="How the figures on this site are made: what counts as a change, how statements are profiled, and how to read the charts, originality scores and shared-wording colours."
>
  <div class="page reading prose stack">
    <h1>Reading the data</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      What each figure on this site measures, and how far to trust it. Start with
      the anatomy of a change: the timeline, the profiles and the charts are all
      built from it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy of a change</h2>
    <p>
      Every night the tracker copies each agency&rsquo;s statement. After a check
      that the copy captured the statement properly, any copy whose text differs
      from the last one is a <em>change</em>. A language model reads each change
      twice: for how much the text changed, and for which of the statement&rsquo;s
      answers changed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both readings record what a statement <em>says</em>, which may differ from
      what an agency does. A statement that doesn&rsquo;t mention a use-case
      register may come from an agency that has one, and a statement that claims
      one may come from an agency that doesn&rsquo;t.
    </p>

    <h3 id="example">An example</h3>
    <p>
      Here is one change as the timeline shows it, from the <a
        href={statementPath(example.abbr)}>{example.agency}</a
      >.
    </p>
    <ul class="example">
      <ChangeFeedItem
        event={example}
        diff={{ before: exampleBefore.body, after: exampleAfter.body, delta: exampleAfter.charDelta }}
        deltas={exampleAfter.profileDeltas}
        open
      />
    </ul>
    <ol class="example-key">
      <li>
        The header gives the date of the copy, the agency and how many characters
        the text gained or lost. The label is the change&rsquo;s <a href="#changes"
          >tier</a
        >: substantive, because reporting an appointment is a new fact.
      </li>
      <li>
        The sentence and the points under it are the first reading: what changed,
        in the model&rsquo;s words. A point starting &ldquo;Removed&rdquo; is shown
        in red.
      </li>
      <li>
        The coloured chips are the second reading: the <a href="#profiles"
          >answers</a
        > that changed. Red (&minus;) is an answer dropped, green (+) one added,
        grey (~) one altered.
      </li>
      <li>
        The words that changed are the diff both readings came from, with deleted
        text struck through in red and inserted text underlined in green.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The red chip says a commitment was dropped, but the diff shows why: the
      promise to appoint a Chief AI Officer was kept. This is why every reading
      sits beside its diff (see <a href="#checking">checking a reading</a>).
    </p>

    <h3 id="changes">How much the text changed</h3>
    <p>
      Each change is put in one of four tiers.
    </p>
    <dl class="tiers">
      <dt>Noise</dt>
      <dd>
        Nothing the agency wrote changed: a sidebar rotated, a &ldquo;last reviewed
        3 days ago&rdquo; counter ticked over, or the page was rebuilt around the
        same words.
      </dd>
      <dt>Cosmetic</dt>
      <dd>Wording, punctuation, formatting or a link changed, but nothing of substance.</dd>
      <dt>Reworded</dt>
      <dd>The text was rewritten or rearranged, but makes the same claims and commitments.</dd>
      <dt>Substantive</dt>
      <dd>A claim, commitment, fact or disclosure was added, removed or altered.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Substantive and reworded changes appear on the timeline and statement pages,
      each with a one-sentence summary and a list of the points that changed.
      &ldquo;Changes of substance&rdquo; counts only the substantive tier. Noise and
      cosmetic changes are hidden unless you ask for them.
    </p>

    <h3 id="profiles">Which answers changed</h3>
    <p>
      The model also reads every version of every statement into a <em>profile</em>:
      a fixed set of questions, each with a fixed set of possible answers. Most of
      the questions come from the three documents behind the statements. The
      DTA&rsquo;s Standard lists eight things every statement must contain, policy
      v2.0 adds five requirements, and the APS AI Plan asks each agency for a Chief
      AI Officer. The rest are ours, and are marked as such.
    </p>
    <p>
      Comparing a version&rsquo;s profile with the one before shows which answers
      were added, dropped or altered, such as &ldquo;Chief AI Officer: planned
      &rarr; in place&rdquo;. A bullet point expanded into a paragraph about the
      same thing changes no answer. Profiles record job titles, never
      people&rsquo;s names.
    </p>

    <h4 id="schema">Where the questions come from</h4>
    <p>
      <a href={withBase("/whos-who")}>Who&rsquo;s who</a> explains how the three
      documents fit together. A question is a requirement only if its source makes
      it one: the Chief AI Officer comes from the AI Plan, not the policy. Usage
      patterns and domains use the DTA&rsquo;s own categories.
    </p>
    <dl class="sources">
      {
        sources.map((source) => (
          <div>
            <dt>
              {FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].href ? (
                <a href={FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].href}>{FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].long}</a>
              ) : (
                FIELD_SOURCE_LABEL[source].long
              )}
            </dt>
            <dd>
              <ul>
                {REQUIREMENTS.filter((q) => q.source === source).map((q) => (
                  <li>{q.question}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h3 id="checking">Checking a reading</h3>
    <p>
      Both readings come from a model, and either can be wrong. When an agency
      rewrites a section, a commitment it only reworded can show as dropped, with
      the new wording shown as added. Most of the dropped commitments on this site
      are of this kind, though some are genuine; the diff beside each one tells
      you which. A vaguely described role can also land in the wrong category.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every summary names the model that wrote it and sits beside the diff, and
      every profile sits beside its statement, so you can check one against the
      other. The <a href={withBase("/data/")}>downloads</a> have every reading in
      bulk.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corpus">When the record starts</h2>
    <p>
      Tracking began on {corpusStart}. Statements have been <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >required since February 2025</a
      >, so their first nine months aren&rsquo;t in the record. Every statement that
      already existed enters on day one as &ldquo;first tracked&rdquo;, because we
      don&rsquo;t know when it was published or how it read before then. If you
      have an earlier version, <a href={withBase("/about#contribute")}>we&rsquo;d
        like to add it</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 id="adoption">Adoption over time</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={withBase("/policy")}>What the statements say</a> charts, for each
      month, the share of tracked statements whose profile reports each item (a
      Chief AI Officer, a register, mandatory training, a reference to policy
      v2.0). Agencies joined the tracker in waves, so a jump between months can
      come from new statements rather than new text, and early months have fewer
      statements ({meta.counts.statements} are tracked now).
    </p>
    <p>
      Two lists on that page use the statements&rsquo; own dates. A statement
      counts as updated since policy v2.0 if its stated last-updated date, or a
      change we saw, falls on or after 15 December 2025. It is listed as not
      updated if we have watched it since before then and seen neither; otherwise
      we can&rsquo;t tell. The Standard asks for a review at least once a year, so
      the second list shows statements whose own date is more than a year old. A
      statement with no date can&rsquo;t appear on it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reuse">Shared wording</h2>
    <p>
      On each statement page, an <em>amber</em> tint marks a passage that also
      appears in other agencies&rsquo; statements. The deeper the amber, the more
      agencies share it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>uncoloured</strong>: only in this statement;</li>
      <li><strong>a few</strong>: shared with two or three other agencies;</li>
      <li><strong>several</strong>: shared with four to nine;</li>
      <li><strong>many</strong>: shared with ten to twenty-four;</li>
      <li><strong>most</strong>: shared with twenty-five or more.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Open the small count on an amber passage to see which agencies share it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <em>grey</em> tint marks wording from the policy or the DTA&rsquo;s <a
        href="https://www.digital.gov.au/ai/ai-in-government-policy/standard-ai-transparency-statements"
        >template</a
      >, which most agencies start from. It has no count, because it can&rsquo;t be
      traced to particular agencies.
    </p>

    <h3 id="first-observed">First observed</h3>
    <p>
      For each shared passage we record which statement had it first and the order
      in which others picked it up. How far that order can be trusted puts each
      passage in one of three tiers:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>added during tracking</strong> ({tierCounts.added}): we saw the
        first agency add it after tracking began, and others follow. This is the
        strongest evidence of order.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>present at the start</strong> ({tierCounts["present-at-start"]}):
        the first agency already had it when tracking began, and others added it
        later.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>tied</strong> ({tierCounts.tied}): several agencies had it on the
        same earliest date, usually day one, so there is no order.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This is the order we saw, and it doesn&rsquo;t show who wrote a passage. The
      source could be an agency we don&rsquo;t track, a draft that never went
      online, or the DTA template.
    </p>

    <h2 id="originality">Originality scores</h2>
    <p>
      A statement&rsquo;s originality score is the share of its text that is
      neither shared with other agencies nor from the template. A statement at 80%
      is mostly in its own words; one at 20% is mostly shared. The leaderboard and
      the agency grid colour statements on this scale, from borrowed to bespoke.
    </p>
    <p>
      The score doesn&rsquo;t measure effort. Shared text includes wording the
      Standard asks for (the title, the section headings, the OECD definition of
      an AI system), so following the Standard closely lowers the score. A
      statement published jointly by two bodies, such as the AASB and AUASB,
      counts as shared with itself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="more">Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      A capture sometimes picks up parts of the page around the statement. The
      model usually tells the two apart, but not always, so check the source link
      on any statement. The tracker is <a
        href="https://github.com/ANUcybernetics/aps-ai-tracker">open source</a>, for
      anyone who wants the technical method.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .reading {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .reading h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
  }

  .reading h4 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
  }

  .example {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }

  .example :global(.tl-row) {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .example-key {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .example-key li + li {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-1);
  }

  .tiers {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: max-content 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
  }

  .tiers dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .tiers dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (width < 34rem) {
    .tiers {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      row-gap: 0;
    }

    .tiers dd {
      margin-block-end: var(--space-2);
    }
  }

  .sources {
    margin: var(--space-3) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .sources dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .sources dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .sources ul {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
